<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Pomerania-Wolgast</h1><p class="st-meta">1295 CE – 1625 CE (approx.) · capital Wolgast</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The western of the two Pomeranian duchies created by the Griffin partition of 1295, ruled from the castle of Wolgast near the Baltic coast.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1295 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-griffins">Griffin</a> brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-iv-duke-of-pomerania">Bogislaw IV</a> and Otto I divided the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania</a>. Bogislaw took the coastal lands north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peene">Peene</a> and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolgast">Wolgast</a>. In 1325 his line gained the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-rugen">Principality of Rügen</a>, and later partitions split off further branches, including the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-pomerania-stolp">Duchy of Pomerania-Stolp</a> in 1368.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-griffins">Griffins</a> were a Slavic dynasty that had become thoroughly German in culture. Power was shared with the estates and with Hanseatic towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greifswald">Greifswald</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anklam">Anklam</a>, which grew rich on Baltic trade, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-greifswald">Greifswald University</a> was founded in 1456. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-bugenhagen">Johannes Bugenhagen</a>, a Pomeranian, helped bring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Lutheran Reformation</a>, adopted at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-treptow">Diet of Treptow</a> in 1534.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The duchy was reunited with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-x">Bogislaw X</a> from 1478 and split off again in 1532. When Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-julius-duke-of-pomerania-wolgast">Philip Julius</a> died childless in 1625, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-xiv">Bogislaw XIV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania-stettin">Pomerania-Stettin</a> inherited it, uniting <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Pomerania</a> for the last time shortly before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> occupied it in 1630; most of it later became <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/swedish-pomerania">Swedish Pomerania</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-pomerania-wolgast.jpg" alt="Duchy of Pomerania-Wolgast" width="320" height="194"><figcaption>Image: Gustav Droysen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cammin_prince-bishopric_14th_century_(134037786).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1295 CE – 1625 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wolgast</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bogislaw IV, Wartislaw IV, Philip I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, then Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pommern-Wolgast</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1460&amp;focus=duchy-of-pomerania-wolgast" aria-label="Duchy of Pomerania-Wolgast on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4985 323 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Pomerania-Wolgast lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5305 579L5296 569M5305 579L5324 597M5305 579L5376 568"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5296 569h0M5324 597h0M5376 568h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5305 579h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5305 579h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Wolgast. <a href="/atlas?year=1460&amp;focus=duchy-of-pomerania-wolgast">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
